{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q12",
  "speechText": "For me, I would start with the user goal and keep the agent inside a clear workflow. It should only work with authorized context. That's because I would not send the whole database to the model. I would only retrieve the relevant and authorized context for that user. For example, if a user asks about training progress, the agent may need the user profile, recent workouts, goals, and progress metrics. Then I would expose a small set of backend tools. The model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, and checks permission before doing it. This is similar to what I worked with in Clincove, where the agent could search and read documents through controlled backend tools. The backend kept control of the access scope and permissions. For tracking, I keep clear logs with the important IDs, status, errors, and performance metrics, such as latency, processing time, and memory usage. This helps me monitor the system, find what went wrong, and debug problems more easily. I would also use retry and recovery where needed and add regression and eval cases for important workflows. So overall, the model can decide which tool it needs. But the backend still defines the tool contract, validates the arguments, and checks permission before doing it.",
